synopsis:
The Lusiads is a work of epic poetry by the Portuguese writer Luís Vaz de Camões, the first Portuguese epic published in printed form. Probably begun in 1556 and completed in 1571, it was published in Lisbon on March 12, 1572, in the literary period of Classicism, or the late Renaissance, three years after the author's return from the East, via Mozambique.

The work is composed of ten cantos, 1,102 strophes, also known as stanzas, and 8,816 lines in decasyllabic octaves, subject to the fixed rhyme scheme ABABABCC – royal, or Camonian, ottava rima. The central action is the discovery of the sea route to India by Vasco da Gama, around which other episodes from the history of Portugal are evoked, glorifying the Portuguese people.

The external structure refers to the formal analysis of the poem: number of stanzas, number of lines per stanza, number of metrical syllables, types of rhyme, rhythm, figures of speech, etc. Thus:

The Lusiads consists of ten parts, called cantos in lyric poetry;

each canto has a variable number of stanzas (on average, 110);

the stanzas are octaves, thus having eight lines; the rhyme is alternating in the first six lines and paired in the last two (AB AB AB CC, see the quotation alongside);

each line consists of ten metrical syllables (decasyllabic), most of them heroic (stressed on the sixth and tenth syllables).

As The Lusiads is a Renaissance text, it could not fail to follow the Greek aesthetic that gave particular importance to the golden number. Thus, the climax of the narrative, the arrival in India, was placed at the point that divides the work according to the golden ratio (beginning of Canto VII).

The internal structure relates to the content of the text.

Madame Bovary is a novel by Gustave Flaubert. Called the "novel of novels", Madame Bovary is considered a pioneer among realist novels, becoming famous for its originality. It later led to the coining of the term "bovarism" in psychology, in reference to the psychological traits of the protagonist. When it was released, Flaubert was put on trial for the work, arousing great interest in the novel.

Part I

1- Charles Bovary's childhood: the student's day;

2- First marriage. Charles meets Rouault and his daughter Emma; Charles's first wife dies;

3- Charles asks for Emma's hand in marriage;

4- The wedding;

5- The new home in Tostes;

6- An account of Emma's childhood and her secret fantasy world;

7- Emma grows bored; invitation to a ball from the Marquis d'Andervilliers;

8- The ball at the Château La Vaubyessard;

9- Emma follows fashions, complains of boredom to Charles, and they decide to move; they discover that she is pregnant;

Part II

1- Description of Yonville-l'Abbaye: Homais, Lestiboudois, Binet, Bournisien, Lheureux

2- Emma meets Léon Dupuis, the lawyer's clerk

3- Emma gives birth to Berta, the visit to the wet nurse's house with Léon

4- A game of cards; Emma's friendship with Léon grows

5- Trip to see the flax; Emma is resigned to her life

6- Emma visits the priest Bournisien; Berta is injured; Léon travels to Paris

7- Charles's mother forbids her to read; the bloodletting of Rodolphe's farmhand; Rodolphe meets Emma

8- The agricultural show; Rodolphe courts Emma

9- Six weeks
